# a-tower-buries-its-own-wall — plan 12 B7, and the phase's finding.
#
# `a-defended-base.keys` plus ONE marker: a tower at (3, 0), one hex
# behind the wall it covers.  Everything else is identical.
#
#   undefended:         61 ticks   ( 41 s)
#   sealed wall:       104 ticks   ( 69 s)
#   this file:          95 ticks   ( 63 s)  — the tower gives back 9
#
# ⚠⚠ **The tower STILL makes the base fall sooner, and it is not a
# bug.**  It is `docs/ENEMY_MOVEMENT.md` § Bodies are terrain arriving
# exactly as written, in a base with nobody to clear up: *"enemies climb
# their own dead onto the wall"*.  The tower kills eight robots at the
# foot of its own wall; three of them fall on (6, 0) and make a 1.5 m
# step; a robot climbs 2.0, and 1.5 + 2.0 clears a 3.0 m wall.  The
# survivors walk over their dead and into the base, and the wall never
# loses a tenth of its HP.
#
# ⚠ **Plan 11 F7b shrank the penalty from ruinous to a rounding.**  On
# the queueing mover this file read 180 ticks against the wall's 311 —
# the tower gave back 131 of the 150 the wall had bought.  With the
# sidestep it gives back 9 of 43.  Both facts have the same cause: the
# body ramp is a fixed prize, and it used to be measured against a clock
# the queue had inflated threefold.  The tower is still a net loss for
# an unattended base, and it is now a small one.
#
# B0 derived that band before anything could die: a single-hex body ramp
# onto a structure `H` high needs a climb of `H / 2`, so onto a `wall`
# the workable pile is [1.0, 2.0] m — *"a couple of dead robots get the
# next one over"*.  This is the first time it has fired in anger, and it
# fires against the player.
#
# ── The ending this one reaches: THE PILE WENT OVER ─────────────
#
# The other of the two `plans/12` asks B7 to distinguish.  The wall is
# INTACT — its middle at 97 of 100 HP and its end still standing with
# 2.7 to spare — and the base fell anyway.  `a-defended-base.keys` is the wall breaking;
# which ending you get is decided by whether you built a tower.
#
# ⚠ **What this does NOT say is that towers are bad.**  It says a tower
# without a cleanup crew is, and the crew is the vehicle plan 12 does
# not build.  Salvage decays, bodies have to be collected at the worst
# possible moment, and `CLAUDE.md` § What dryopea is calls that the
# design's whole test — *does this put something in the player's hands
# at a moment when using it costs them something?*  This file is that
# question with the player removed, and the answer is the base.

echo a tower buries its own wall: eight kills, a body ramp, and a shorter clock

do toggle_mode

do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

# The tower, one hex behind the wall.
# ⚠ **ONE hex, and B5b measured why.**  A shot descends from the eye at
# 6.0 m to a robot's body at 1.0 m, so a 3 m wall stops it once it
# stands past ~3/5 of the way to the target: a tower one hex behind its
# wall covers the besiegers, and two hexes back leaves them in dead
# ground.  A tower at the core would see nothing at all.
do cycle_kind
click 3 0
marker 3 0 tower

do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 11 0
marker 11 0 spawn 3

count markers 3 3
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag -3 -2 12 -2
drag -3 -1 12 -1
drag -3 0 12 0
drag -3 1 12 1
drag -3 2 12 2
count painted 80 80

palette 9
click 5 -2
click 5 -1
click 5 0
click 5 1
click 5 2
kind 5 0 wall
hp 5 0 100.0 100.0

wave 5
count alive 5 5
wallet 200.0 200.0

tick 22
wave 8

# ⚠ A tower fires one shot a second out of a 30-shot budget, so it is
# spending that budget from the moment the first wave walks into range
# — and every kill is a body nobody will collect.
#
# ⚠ It read **10** until plan 24 W1 widened the siege front.  The
# besiegers now stand spread along the wall's face instead of stacked
# behind the desire gradient's minimum, so the hexes the tower's single
# sight line reaches are not the same hexes — and at this tick that is
# one kill fewer.  ⚠ A positional consequence, not a change to the
# tower: nothing in `tower.loft` moved.
count alive 11 11

fall 800
ticks 123 133
wallet 0.0 0.0

# ── ⚠⚠ The ending CHANGED, and the file's name is now a scar ────
#
# **Plan 16 W2's pre-walk window inverted this scenario.**  Before it,
# this base fell in 95 ticks where the same base without a tower held
# 104 — the tower's own kills piled 1.5 m at the wall's foot and the
# survivors walked in over them.  That was plan 12 B7's headline: *a
# tower CUTS the clock, because its bodies ramp over the wall it was
# defending.*
#
# With the window it is the other way round.  A wave stands 8 ticks at
# its marker, so the tower's first kills fall out THERE instead of at
# the wall — and a `wall` at 3 m hides the rest from a 6 m eye until
# they close (plan 12 B5b), so the tower lands fewer kills at the foot
# of the wall than it used to.  No ramp forms at all.
#
#     with a tower, no window:    95 ticks   <- the tower CUT it
#     with a tower, this file:   128 ticks   <- the tower BUYS time
#     no tower (a-defended-base) 112 ticks
#
# ⚠ So the mechanic is not deleted — it is CONDITIONAL on where the
# kills land, which is a property of the map and the window rather than
# of towers.  The file keeps its name because the mechanic it is named
# for is the thing it now measures the ABSENCE of, and a scenario that
# can go either way is worth more than one that only ever showed the
# bad half.  `plans/16` § W2 carries the finding.

# The wall did the work this time, and it lost its end FIRST doing it —
# exactly the ending `a-defended-base.keys` reaches.
#
# ⚠ **Since plan 24 W1 the middle goes too, and that is saturation
# rather than a lost property.**  The front is the face's width now, so
# a five-hex wall takes about half again as much damage over the same
# run and nothing on it survives to the fall.  The ORDER is unchanged —
# the 30 HP end still runs out long before the 100 HP middle — and it
# is gated where it belongs, in `tests/11_f7b_the_sidestep.loft` and
# `tests/12_b3_bracing.loft`, which measure WHICH hex goes first
# instead of photographing the wreckage afterwards.
kind 5 -2 grass
kind 5 0 grass

# And the middle left a heap of its own, so the perimeter has two ways
# through it — the same ending `a-defended-base.keys` reaches.
pile 5 0 0.9 1.1

# ⚠⚠ **THE ASSERTION THIS FILE NOW EXISTS FOR: no ramp.**  Three dead
# robots at the wall's foot were what got them in; there are none.
pile 6 0 0.0 0.0

# ⚠ **And the tower is now worth 16 ticks rather than costing 9.**
# Five of thirteen still walked in — through the breach, not over a
# heap.
count alive 5 5

snap b7_a_tower_buries_its_own_wall
